C = [
("h1", "12. The Man Christ Jesus"),
("p", "Claim: the same Paul who calls Christ God calls him a man, born of a woman and of the seed of David. The two sayings are made by one writer and the volume does not play them against each other. Each is stated as fact, and neither cancels the other."),
("sub", "12.1 The passages"),
("p", "“For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus; Who gave himself a ransom for all, to be testified in due time” (1 Timothy 2:5, 6). “But when the fulness of the time was come, God sent forth his Son, made of a woman, made under the law” (Galatians 4:4). “Concerning his Son Jesus Christ our Lord, which was made of the seed of David according to the flesh; And declared to be the Son of God with power, according to the spirit of holiness, by the resurrection from the dead” (Romans 1:3, 4)."),
("sub", "12.2 What each says"),
("ctbl", "The man in Paul’s letters.", [
    ("The passage", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("1 Timothy 2:5", "One God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus", "How the mediator is one with the one God; the verse sets them in one sentence"),
    ("Galatians 4:4", "God sent forth his Son, made of a woman, made under the law", "The Son’s state before he was sent; the verb “sent forth” speaks of one who was already there"),
    ("Romans 1:3, 4", "Of the seed of David according to the flesh; declared the Son of God with power by the resurrection", "Whether the declaration made him Son or showed that he was"),
    ("Philippians 2:7, 8", "Made in the likeness of men; found in fashion as a man", "What “likeness” excludes"),
    ("2 Corinthians 8:9", "Though he was rich, yet for your sakes he became poor", "What the riches were; the verse does not name them"),
  ], [0.24, 0.40, 0.36]),
("sub", "12.3 “God sent forth his Son”"),
("p", "The verb in Galatians 4:4 is “sent forth.” A person who is sent forth is already there to be sent. The verse then adds two states into which he was sent: “made of a woman, made under the law.” The Son is the subject both before and after. The verse is used in the volume on Israel and the Body for what it says of the law, and here it is read only for what it says of the Son: he was sent, and he was made of a woman."),
("sub", "12.4 Declared the Son of God with power"),
("p", "Romans 1:3 and 1:4 set two things side by side. The first is that he was “made of the seed of David according to the flesh.” The second is that he was “declared to be the Son of God with power, according to the spirit of holiness, by the resurrection from the dead.” The word is “declared.” The verse says that the resurrection declared what he was and does not say that the resurrection made him so. The volume reads it as written. It does not say that the sentence means he became Son at the resurrection, and it does not say that the verse teaches he was already the Son before it. The sentence states the declaration, and the point of the declaration is the power."),
("sub", "12.5 “Though he was rich”"),
("p", "“For ye know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that, though he was rich, yet for your sakes he became poor, that ye through his poverty might be rich” (2 Corinthians 8:9). The verse is written to ask for a gift, and it uses Christ as the example. It says that he was rich and became poor. It does not say what the riches were. The volume notes that the sentence has the same movement as Philippians 2: one who was in a high state, and who for the sake of others took a lower. The two letters do not use the same words, and the volume does not make them one passage."),
("sub", "12.6 One writer, two sayings"),
("p", "A reader who meets these sayings together may think that one must give way to the other. The sayings of Paul about the man are not the retreat from the sayings about God. He writes “the man Christ Jesus” in the same letter as “God was manifest in the flesh.” He writes “made of a woman” in the same chapter in which he writes of God sending his Son. He writes “as concerning the flesh Christ came” in the same sentence as “God blessed for ever.” The pairing is Paul’s own, and it occurs in letters to different churches. The volume does not say how the man and God are one. It says that the writer says both, and that no one has the right to remove either."),
("sub", "12.7 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Paul calls Christ a man, born of a woman and of the seed of David, and the one mediator between God and men.",
    "Galatians 4:4 says God sent forth his Son, which presupposes a Son who was there to be sent.",
    "Romans 1:4 says he was declared Son of God with power by the resurrection, and does not say he became Son by it.",
    "The pairing of man and God occurs within single letters and sentences, and the volume does not cancel either side.",
  ]),
("p", "The next chapter takes the Father and the Son, and the three after it gather the sayings: clause by clause, side by side, and by what they leave unsaid."),
]
